# qemu-tool settings for a NIXL + MORI test cluster.
#
# An N-VM rocm-ernic mesh, one emulated RDMA NIC and one rocjitsu GPU per VM,
# each VM with a local NVMe. Enough to exercise the NIXL backends that do not
# need real hardware: UCX and LIBFABRIC between guests over the emulated ERNIC
# port, POSIX and the AIS family against the local NVMe.
#
# This file is an overlay on qemu-minimal's own `scale-out` profile, which is
# the authoritative one -- it is layered after it, and only carries the keys
# that differ. Render and bring up with:
#
#   QM=~/Projects/qemu-minimal
#   qemu-tool gen-compose \
#       --env-file $QM/qemu/env/scale-out \
#       --env-file cluster/env/nixl-mori \
#       --output-dir /data/qemu-tool-stebates/stacks/nixl-mori
#   qemu-tool compose --stack-dir /data/qemu-tool-stebates/stacks/nixl-mori \
#       --env-file $QM/qemu/env/scale-out \
#       --env-file cluster/env/nixl-mori up -d
#
# `make cluster-up` does both. Both directory flags are load-bearing: without
# --output-dir the render lands on qemu-minimal's committed stack, and without
# --stack-dir the compose run brings up that stack rather than this one.
#
# ---- What each VM gets ------------------------------------------------------
#
# Two containers per VM beyond QEMU itself, and the order they start in
# matters:
#
#   ernic-N     the emulated RDMA NIC, serving a vfio-user socket.
#   tapsetup-N  joins ernic-N's *network namespace*, creates tap0 there, exits.
#
# ernic-N waits for tap0 before it execs, so restarting ernic-N alone strands
# it: the restart gives it a fresh namespace with no tap0 and it dies after
# two minutes with "tap0 never appeared". Start tapsetup-N again right after.
# Restarting ernic-N also recreates its vfio-user socket, so the guest loses
# its device and qemu-N has to be restarted too.
#
# rocjitsu-N leaves a stale socket behind when it stops and cannot rebind to
# it; delete /run/vfu/rocjitsu-N.sock before restarting it.

VM_COUNT=4
VM_NAME_PREFIX=nixl-mori

# ---- Images -----------------------------------------------------------------
#
# All four moved together to 20261030.gadc94af. qemu-tool asserts that they
# share one <date>.<ci-sha>, and that QEMU_IMAGE and ERNIC_IMAGE share one
# vfu.* revision, because a guest driver and a host rocm-ernic from different
# SHAs do not fail cleanly across the vfio-user boundary. Do not bump one.
#
# The backing image is the reason for the move. scale-out pins the
# `ernic-rocjitsu` flavour and this cluster was running `ionic` instead --
# the plain NIC guest, with `"dkms": []` and no ROCm. That image has only the
# in-tree mainline amdgpu, which cannot bind the emulated gfx1250: it fails in
# IP discovery at `Failed to add vcn/jpeg ip block(UVD_HWIP:0x0)` and probes
# -EINVAL. No amdgpu-probe parameter reaches that -- the failure is upstream of
# ip_block_mask -- and the firmware is not the problem either: the guest's
# ip_discovery.bin is byte-identical to one regenerated from the rocjitsu build
# serving the device. What the ernic-rocjitsu flavour has and the ionic one
# does not is amdgpu-dkms 7.1.9, which is the driver that tolerates that table.
#
# Without a GPU, MORI_IO cannot run at all: mori::io builds its topology
# through ROCm-SMI before any transport starts and calls exit(-1) on zero GPUs,
# so even a DRAM-segment run aborts. The GPU is not optional here for the same
# reason the benchmark exists.
#
# This flavour also carries ernic-p4 and ionic-ernic as DKMS modules rather
# than relying on the in-tree ionic.
# ...with two held back, and this is a real version skew, not caution. The
# 20261030 rocm-ernic binds its mesh-manager port on tcp6 only -- `[::]:1978`,
# nothing in /proc/net/tcp -- and gen-compose's manager healthcheck greps
# /proc/net/tcp for a v4 LISTEN. It never passes, every worker gates on it,
# and no qemu-N starts, so the whole stack fails to come up on an ernic that
# is in fact running correctly. qemu-tool 1.4.1 still generates the v4-only
# check, so this cannot be fixed by moving qemu-tool forward.
#
# QEMU_IMAGE comes back with it because the two must share a vfu.* revision.
# The FORK, not stock QEMU, and this is what makes VRAM work over the fabric.
# An RDMA write into GPU memory is one vfio-user device (the ernic) DMAing
# into another's BAR (the rocjitsu GPU). Stock QEMU cannot do vfio-user P2P
# DMA: vfio_user_dma_map skips sending an fd in DMA_MAP when
# memory_region_get_fd() returns -1, and for vfio-user mmap regions the
# backing fd arrives from GET_REGION_INFO but is never propagated to the
# RAMBlock, so the GPU BAR window is never advertised to peer vfio-user
# servers. It does not report this -- the registration succeeds and the
# transfer simply fails.
#
# qemu.aa4344e carries the fix (hw/vfio/region.c, "enabling peer P2P DMA").
# scale-out's comment that NO published image contains it is out of date; it
# was written against qemu.d757fa6, which does not. aa4344e shares both the
# 20261021.g742e8e2 build stamp and the vfu.8039244 revision with ERNIC_IMAGE,
# so the coherence check still passes.
QEMU_IMAGE=docker.io/sbates130272/batesste-ci-images-ubuntu-qemu-libvfio-user-sbates-fork:20261021.g742e8e2-qemu.aa4344e-vfu.8039244
# Our own build, not a published one. The published ernic at this build stamp
# predates ROCm/rocm-ernic#315, so guest-to-guest rdma_cm does not work on it
# and NIXL's LIBFABRIC backend hangs rather than transfers. Every published
# build that DOES carry the fix is at a libvfio-user revision the forked QEMU
# is not, and the vfio-user wire protocol is not compatible across revisions.
#
# docker/rocm-ernic/Dockerfile resolves that by building a newer server
# against this image's own libvfio-user. Build it with `make ernic-image`.
#
# It has no build stamp, so gen-compose's coherence check warns and excludes
# it rather than refusing -- which is the behaviour that check documents for a
# locally built image carrying a fix under test.
ERNIC_IMAGE=nixl-mori-ernic:051ff6d
ROCJITSU_IMAGE=docker.io/sbates130272/batesste-ci-images-ubuntu-rocm-rocjitsu:20261021.g742e8e2-rocjitsu.84fb06b

# So the three vfio-user servers stay on one stamp and only the guest moves.
# Safe for the GPU specifically: `vfio_guest_firmware.py --generation gfx1250`
# emits a byte-identical ip_discovery.bin from rocjitsu.84fb06b and
# rocjitsu.73c3cf3, so the table baked into the 20261030 guest matches what
# the 20261021 server presents.
#
# Read the flag's own docstring before trusting it further than that. It
# argues the guest is excusable because ubuntu-qcow2-gen installs no driver
# and no rdma-core -- true of the `ionic` flavour, NOT of `ernic-rocjitsu`,
# which ships amdgpu, ionic-ernic and ernic-p4 as DKMS modules. So this is a
# real guest/server skew on the RDMA side, and it is also backwards from the
# case the flag was written for: here the guest is newer than the servers.
# If the fabric starts reporting DSR -ETIMEDOUT or "Failed to register ibdev",
# this is the first thing to suspect.
IMAGE_ALLOW_GUEST_LAG=true
VM_BACKING_IMAGE=docker.io/sbates130272/batesste-ci-images-ubuntu-qcow2-gen-ernic-rocjitsu:20261030.gadc94af-vm.resolute-ernic-rocjitsu-qm.ae551b3-qcow2

# Not qemu-minimal's own images directory. The fleet SSH key this cluster
# reaches its guests with lives here, alongside the prepared backing image,
# and gen-vm bakes that key into whatever it builds -- so pointing elsewhere
# produces guests nothing can log into.
#
# Under /data, not /var/lib: recreating anything under /var/lib needs root,
# and this tree was deleted out from under a running cluster once already.
# /home is not an option either -- it is NFS, and qcow2 images backing live
# VMs want local disk. /data is local ext4 and writable without sudo.
VM_IMAGES_DIR=/data/qemu-tool-stebates/images

# Where qemu-tool itself comes from. The qemu container installs it from this
# tree when the path holds a pyproject.toml, and falls back to the published
# package otherwise -- and the published one is behind: it has no
# --vfio-user-root-port, so every VM exits 2 before QEMU starts. The default
# is a relative path that does not resolve from an absolute --output-dir.
QEMU_TOOL_SRC=/home/AMD/stebates/Projects/qemu-minimal

# Non-colliding with qemu-minimal's own fleets. Change all of these together
# if two clusters have to coexist on one host.
FLEET_NET_PREFIX=172.26
ERNIC_TCP_PORT=6520
VM_SSH_PORT_BASE=2400
ERNIC_MAC_PREFIX=72:6f:63:6e
# Must fall INSIDE the subnet docker assigns the stack's `l2` network, which
# is the bridge the guests' tap devices join. That network is declared with no
# IPAM config, so docker picks the subnet itself -- 192.168.224.0/20 here.
# Guests outside it ARP fine (not IP-filtered) and then have every unicast
# packet dropped by docker's FORWARD rules, which looks like a dead fabric
# with a healthy link. Check `docker network inspect <stack>_l2` before
# changing this.
ERNIC_GUEST_SUBNET=192.168.80
ERNIC_STATS_PORT=9870
PROM_PORT=9290

# Put each vfio-user device behind its own pcie-root-port. qemu-minimal's
# scale-out profile does this and its overlay-mi100 turns it off, for a reason
# local to that fleet -- it did not want a PCI-topology change landing at the
# same time as a new drive. Inheriting the "off" here is wrong: without a root
# port a device is a conventional PCI endpoint with no Express capability, so
# it gets a 32-bit prefetchable BAR. The emulated GPU then comes up with a
# 16 MB BAR0, amdgpu's memory controller cannot map VRAM through a window that
# small, and the probe fails -EINVAL with no KFD device -- which takes the AIS
# and AIS_MT backends with it.
VM_VFIO_USER_ROOT_PORT=true

# Two NICs and two GPUs per guest, so there is more than one path to test:
# a second rail to stripe or fail over to, and a second device to move memory
# between without crossing the fabric at all.
#
# ERNIC_PER_VM multiplies MESH NODES, not just containers -- each rocm-ernic
# is its own node. 4 VMs x 2 is 8, comfortably under the ~40 the mesh has been
# measured to register, but it does double the emulator processes competing
# for this host's 32 cores, which are already 2x oversubscribed by the guests
# alone. Expect per-pair throughput to fall even where nothing is wrong.
ERNIC_PER_VM=2
ROCJITSU_PER_VM=2

# The scale-out profile's default, gfx1250_mi455x.json, is not drivable by the
# guest's amdgpu. It carries no `ip_versions` table, so IP discovery hands the
# driver UVD_HWIP 0x0, amdgpu logs "Failed to add vcn/jpeg ip block" and the
# probe dies -EINVAL -- late enough to look like a firmware problem (the gfx
# and sdma blocks are detected first) and early enough that no /dev/kfd node
# for the device is ever created. rocm-smi then reports 0 GPUs, which takes
# MORI_IO with it: mori::io builds its topology through ROCm-SMI before any
# transport starts, so even a DRAM-segment run aborts at
# RSMI_STATUS_INIT_ERROR.
#
# The `_kmd` configs are the ones meant to be driven by a kernel-mode driver
# and do carry ip_versions. gfx942 rather than gfx950 or gfx1250 because the
# guest kernel and the ROCm in the image both support CDNA3 without argument;
# the point here is a GPU that probes, not a specific architecture.
ROCJITSU_CONFIG=gfx1250_mi455x.json

# Two local NVMe per guest, which is what the POSIX and AIS backends are
# pointed at. They arrive unformatted: `make cluster-nvme` makes an ext4 on
# each and mounts them at /mnt/nvme0 and /mnt/nvme1. Two rather than one so a
# storage run can be driven across separate devices rather than contending on
# a single queue.
VM_NVME=2

# 7 GB is the qemu-minimal default and it is tight: nixlbench allocates
# --total_buffer_size per process, default 8 GiB, which does not fit. 16 GiB
# leaves room for the default buffer plus the GPU and storage paths; the
# cluster targets still pass a smaller buffer for most runs.
VM_VMEM=16384
VM_VCPUS=8

GRAFANA_ENABLE=false
LOGS_ENABLE=false
LMCACHE_ENABLE=false
VM_EXTRA_HOSTFWD=
PROM_DATA_DIR=/data/qemu-tool-stebates/stacks/nixl-mori/prom-data
